300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (We were all someone's child before we became anything else: a name on a class list, a height marked quietly on a doorframe, a future no one had asked us about yet. Childhood does not announce itself as something we will one day mourn. It simply happens, in scraped knees and borrowed bicycles, in afternoons that felt endless because we did not yet know what it meant for time to run out.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (The apple becomes a mountain because that is what childhood does to memory: it takes the smallest, most ordinary objects, a fruit, a song, a particular slant of light through a window, and turns them into landmarks too large to ignore. We did not choose what we would remember. Memory chose for us, and we have spent the years since trying to understand why.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (In these pages are letters to who we used to be, written by people who have carried their younger selves quietly for a long time, sometimes with tenderness, sometimes with the discomfort of recognizing how much has changed and how much, somehow, has not. There is no single way to look back. Some do it with grief. Some with gratitude. Most with both, tangled together the way they tend to be.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
